# Isolated Composite Groups & Layer Masking

This guide describes how to use the custom isolated composite groups, blending, and layer masking system in your MapLibre styles.

By rendering layers together in an offscreen framebuffer before compositing them onto the map, you can apply complex graphic design blend modes (e.g., `multiply`, `screen`, `overlay`) and vector layer masks without interference from the map background.

---

## Key Core Properties

To implement composition and masking, three properties are added to the style layer specification:

| Property | Level | Type | Description |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **`composite-group`** | Layer Root | `string` | An arbitrary identifier. All layers sharing the same `composite-group` string will render to an isolated offscreen buffer. |
| **`composite-mask`** | Layer Root | `boolean` | If set to `true`, this layer acts as an alpha clipping mask for all other (non-mask) layers inside the same group. |
| **`<type>-blend-mode`** | Layer Paint | `enum` | Defines how the layer blends with other layers *inside* the composite group. E.g., `"fill-blend-mode"` or `"line-blend-mode"`. |

### Supported Blend Modes
Inside a `composite-group`, you can use standard blend modes or Porter-Duff compositing operators:
* `normal` (default)
* `multiply`, `screen`, `overlay`, `darken`, `lighten`
* `source-in`, `source-out`, `source-atop`
* `destination-in`, `destination-out`
* **`mask`** (Path B: Acts as a specialized alias to isolate the layer as a robust, order-independent clipping mask)

---

## How Masking Works

There are two primary ways to clip a group of layers using a vector shape:

### Approach A: The `"mask"` Blend Mode (Order-Independent)
Setting the layer's blend mode to `"mask"` (e.g. `"fill-blend-mode": "mask"`) automatically designates the layer as a clipping mask.

* **Advantage:** The mask layer can be placed **anywhere in your style sheet stack** (even at the bottom of the group list). The rendering engine automatically identifies it, separates it into a distinct pass, and clips the rest of the group to its alpha profile.

### Approach B: Standard `"destination-in"` Blend Mode (Strict Ordering)
If you do not want the engine to use a separate offscreen mask buffer, you can use `"fill-blend-mode": "destination-in"`.

* **Requirement:** The mask layer **must be placed at the very top of your group** (rendered last). It will clip whatever has already been drawn in the buffer directly beneath it.

